(*
Issue #550: parsePower's right-associative recursion for a chain of
'**'/'pow' ('2 ** 2 ** 2 ** ...') is the one re-entry into expression
parsing that does NOT funnel through parseFactor -- it calls parsePower
directly for its right operand -- so it never bumped ExprDepth (see the
comment on ExprDepth, Parser.h) the way '(' via parseExpression and
'not'/'@' via direct recursion always have. A long enough '**' chain used
to recurse with no bound at all, overflowing the real C++ call stack
DURING PARSING ITSELF, before Sema's own, separately-bounded checkExpr
guard downstream ever got a chance to reject the same expression the way
it already did for an equally long chain of '('.

1000 terms is well past the 500-deep ceiling (MaxExprDepth, ParseExpr.cpp)
while staying fast to parse here; the real crash threshold sits somewhere
between 50,000 (parsed fine, then cleanly rejected by Sema's own guard)
and 100,000 (overflowed the parser's own stack) terms. Checked in verbatim
rather than generated at build time, matching
deeply-nested-parens-report-one-diagnostic-not-a-crash.pas's own precedent
-- lit has no loop-emitting primitive, and the source is deterministic;
nobody should have to look at 1000 terms to read this file, so the lit
directives come first. '-std=iso10206' is required: '**' is Extended
Pascal only.

RUN: not %plang_ir -std=iso10206 -dump-parse-tree %s 2> %t.err
RUN: FileCheck %s < %t.err
RUN: grep -c "error:" %t.err | FileCheck --check-prefix=COUNT --strict-whitespace --match-full-lines %s
*)

